# Target package.use — per-package flags for the image build (plan/03).

# pam: kde-plasma/plasma-login-manager RDEPENDs sys-apps/systemd:=[pam] — it authenticates
# through the systemd PAM stack rather than shipping its own session bookkeeping.
sys-apps/systemd            boot policykit pam
# sudo[sendmail] (default on) pulls virtual/mta so sudo can mail failed-attempt notices; the
# first provider is mail-mta/nullmailer, which drags a mail spool, an nullmail user and an
# sendmail symlink into a desktop image that has no business relaying mail. It also breaks the
# build outright: its pkg_postinst runs "chown nullmail:nullmail" against $ROOT, but chown
# resolves users from the BUILDER's passwd where that user does not exist
# ("chown: invalid user: nullmail"). Off — no MTA in the image (plan/03: nothing like this in
# the native set).
app-admin/sudo              -sendmail
# kernel-open was removed upstream (the open GSP modules are unconditional now), but
# dist-kernel is still a real flag — it comes from linux-mod-r1.eclass, not this ebuild's own
# IUSE, and it is REQUIRED here: without it the ebuild builds modules against a configured
# /usr/src/linux, which an image built from gentoo-kernel-bin does not have ("Unable to find
# kernel sources at $TARGET/usr/src/linux"). With it, modules are built against
# virtual/dist-kernel, which is plan/03's "prebuilt against gentoo-kernel-bin".
# -X: plan/03 is Wayland-only, but nvidia-drivers[X] installs the X DDX (nvidia_drv.so,
# libglx.so) and with it pulls x11-base/xorg-server -> x11-base/xorg-drivers -> every
# xf86-video-* matching VIDEO_CARDS (amdgpu, ati, nouveau) plus xf86-input-libinput and the
# x11-apps client utilities (xinit, xauth, xhost, xset, xrdb, iceauth). A full X server, in an
# image whose session is Wayland-only and whose display manager (Plasma Login Manager) drives
# kwin as its greeter compositor, with no Xorg session installed at all.
# NVIDIA's Wayland path is GBM/EGLStream driven by nvidia-drm.modeset=1, which
# is already on the UKI cmdline in stage 40, and x11-base/xwayland stays in @desktop, so X11
# Flatpak apps keep working. Revert this line if the session ever needs a real Xorg fallback on
# NVIDIA (plan/08 open question 4) — it is the only thing standing between here and that server.
# NB: with GNOME gone this is now one of only TWO -X flags holding the server out, not three;
# kde-plasma/plasma-login-sessions[-X] is the other. See the Plasma session block below.
# -static-libs: .a archives have no consumer in an image with no linker.
x11-drivers/nvidia-drivers  dist-kernel -X -static-libs
sys-kernel/linux-firmware   redistributable -initramfs
media-video/pipewire        sound-server pipewire-alsa
# modemmanager is what kde-plasma/plasma-nm's mobile-broadband UI talks to. Same call plan/03
# made for the GNOME control-center panel: one extra daemon is cheaper than an image that
# cannot bring up a WWAN card at all.
net-misc/networkmanager     wifi modemmanager -bluetooth
media-libs/mesa             vulkan vaapi wayland
# NB: plan/09 listed this line for deletion along with the rest of the GNOME block. That was
# wrong, and the ebuilds say so: media-libs/libcanberra is an UNCONDITIONAL dependency of
# kde-plasma/{kwin,plasma-desktop,plasma-pa,plasma-workspace} — it is the event-sound library
# the whole session uses. It is more firmly in a Plasma image than it ever was in a GNOME one.
# -udev is unchanged and still load-bearing on the BUILDER side: libcanberra's
# REQUIRED_USE="udev? ( alsa )" is evaluated against the builder's own "/", which has no global
# alsa, so a udev-enabled libcanberra reads as unsatisfiable there and fails the target
# depgraph (see the long note in builder/Dockerfile). What udev buys is per-card sound-theme
# lookup, which nothing here asks for.
media-libs/libcanberra      -udev
# installkernel stays inert in the target: no initramfs/bootloader hooks at emerge time —
# the UKI is built from the builder (stage 40), not by package hooks.
sys-kernel/installkernel    -dracut -grub -systemd-boot
# ...and the kernel must not try to build one either. gentoo-kernel-bin[initramfs] (on by
# default) runs a generation step in pkg_postinst that refuses to work with ROOT set:
# "ROOT is set, and (re-)generation of an initramfs is requested via the USE=initramfs flag.
# However, this is currently not supported via the sys-kernel/installkernel mechanism."
# Stage 40 builds the real initrd with dracut --sysroot and folds it into the UKI, so the
# ebuild's copy would be discarded anyway. (-generic-uki for the same reason: our UKI is
# assembled by ukify in stage 40, with our cmdline and os-release.)
sys-kernel/gentoo-kernel-bin -initramfs -generic-uki
# Boot splash. Every glyph and shape on screen is a PNG rasterised at build time from
# config/branding (stage 40, via rsvg-convert), so plymouth needs no text engine of its own:
# -pango drops the label plugin and -freetype the encryption-prompt renderer. -gtk removes the
# X11 renderer, which cannot apply to a UKI booting straight into DRM. drm and udev are the
# renderer and the device discovery this image actually boots on.
# The ESC "details" view was the one behaviour these flags put at risk, since it is plymouthd's
# own plugin rather than the theme's. Verified in QEMU on the shipping image: pressing ESC
# during the splash renders the full systemd boot log with its [ OK ] markers. If a future
# plymouth bump regresses that, turn pango back on — pango, cairo and libpng are already in the
# image for the GTK theming bridge, so it costs no extra packages.
# Version note: 22.02.122-r4 is the amd64 stable; 24.004.60 is ~amd64 AND package-masked
# upstream for breakage, so no package.accept_keywords entry belongs with this.
sys-boot/plymouth           drm udev -pango -freetype -gtk

# ---- Plasma session ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Every flag below was read off the 6.6.6 / 26.04.3 ebuilds in the pinned snapshot, not
# guessed. Eleven of the packages in @desktop have an empty IUSE and correctly appear nowhere
# in this file: systemsettings, breeze, breeze-gtk, kde-gtk-config, xdg-desktop-portal-kde,
# plasma-systemmonitor, plasma-pa, bluedevil, kwallet-pam, polkit-kde-agent and
# plasma-workspace-wallpapers — as do kde-frameworks/baloo and kde-apps/baloo-widgets.
#
# kde-plasma/plasma-login-manager has IUSE="test" only, so it has no line either. What it
# needs from elsewhere is recorded where those flags live: sys-apps/systemd[pam] at the top of
# this file, and kwin[lock] immediately below.

# kwin has NO X flag: kde-plasma/kwin is Wayland-only and the X11 compositor is a separate
# package (kde-plasma/kwin-x11), which nothing here pulls.
# "lock" is NOT default-on, is required by plasma-login-manager, and is the ONLY thing that
# puts a screen locker (kde-plasma/kscreenlocker) in the image. GNOME got the lock screen for
# free inside gnome-shell; here it is one USE flag, and forgetting it ships a laptop that
# cannot lock. Asserted in QEMU (plan/09 §6), because nothing else would catch it.
kde-plasma/kwin                   lock systemd screencast -gamepad -gles2-only -accessibility
# The session .desktop files, and the SECOND of the two remaining -X levers (nvidia-drivers is
# the other). X? RDEPENDs kde-plasma/kwin-x11 — a real X11 session — so this flag, not kwin's
# own, is what keeps the image Wayland-only. REQUIRED_USE="|| ( wayland X )", so wayland is
# written positively rather than relying on its default.
kde-plasma/plasma-login-sessions  wayland -X
# REQUIRED_USE="fontconfig? ( X )" and both are default-on. X here pulls only X *libraries*
# (libX11/libICE/libSM/libXau/libxcb/libXcursor/libXfixes/libXtst + xorg-proto) — no
# xorg-server — which is exactly plan/03's "X11 libraries remain, the Xorg server does not",
# so X stays on and the font-management KCM keeps working.
# semantic-desktop is kde-frameworks/baloo, the image's file indexer. It is written positively
# in all three places it appears, even where it is already the default, so the image keeps its
# indexer if the profile ever drops the token. See the baloo block further down.
# Split across two lines for the same atom deliberately: portage's grabfile() does NOT support
# backslash line continuation, so a trailing "\" would be parsed as a literal USE token.
kde-plasma/plasma-workspace       X fontconfig appstream networkmanager policykit systemd
kde-plasma/plasma-workspace       screencast ksysguard wallpaper-metadata semantic-desktop -telemetry
kde-plasma/plasma-desktop         semantic-desktop -webengine -ibus -scim -sdl
# There is no "packagekit" flag on discover; the PackageKit backend is not built unless
# firmware or snap are. Flatpak backend only, exactly as gnome-software was.
kde-plasma/discover               flatpak -firmware -snap -telemetry -webengine
# brightness-control is DDC/CI for external monitors via app-misc/ddcutil. Laptop backlight
# goes through logind either way, so this buys a package and no function we need.
kde-plasma/powerdevil             -brightness-control
kde-plasma/kscreen                -X
kde-plasma/print-manager          -gtk    # a GTK print dialog module; Flatpaks print via the portal
kde-apps/konsole                  -X
# semantic-desktop here is the Information panel's tags/ratings/comments and content search in
# the Find bar; it is what pulls kde-apps/baloo-widgets. Unlike the two plasma packages above,
# dolphin's own IUSE has it OFF by default — only the profile global turns it on, which is
# exactly why it is spelled out.
kde-apps/dolphin                  semantic-desktop -telemetry
# samba: kept deliberately, for smb:// browsing in Dolphin. This is now the ONLY thing holding
# net-fs/samba in the image, and through it dev-lang/perl + dev-perl/Parse-Yapp (unguarded in
# samba's COMMON_DEPEND). Setting -samba here is the one-flag escape hatch that removes all
# three — see the cascade block below and plan/06.
# taglib: on, because kfilemetadata already pulls media-libs/taglib for the indexer (below),
# so the flag buys audio tags in Dolphin's preview/tooltip path for a package that is free.
kde-apps/kio-extras               samba -mtp -nfs -X taglib -openexr -ios

# The indexer's extractor set — the only real configuration surface Baloo has, since
# kde-frameworks/baloo itself has an empty IUSE. exif and pdf arrive from the profile's global
# USE and are restated; the other four are off by default and are decided here.
# ffmpeg costs nothing: media-video/ffmpeg is already in the image, so it is free video/audio
# metadata. taglib is ~1 MB and covers a music library, which is the case file search is most
# often wanted for. epub would pull app-text/ebook-tools and mobi kde-apps/kdegraphics-
# mobipocket — new packages, for formats plan/03 sends to Flatpak readers.
kde-frameworks/kfilemetadata      exif pdf ffmpeg taglib -epub -mobi

# ---- Frameworks/Qt flags the depgraph requires ---------------------------------------
# Not choices — these are the USE changes "emerge -p @desktop" demands before it will resolve,
# recorded here with the package that demands them rather than pasted from emerge's output.
# kde-frameworks/kio needs qtbase[libproxy,icu] (proxy resolution and collation in the KIO
# slaves) and kwindowsystem[X,wayland] (it talks to both display protocols); kded needs
# kconfig[dbus]. kio arrives via kcmutils -> keditfiletype, i.e. from systemsettings, so this
# is the settings-module half of the graph, not something optional.
# wayland+opengl: kde-frameworks/kwindowsystem[wayland] requires qtbase[wayland], and qtbase's
# REQUIRED_USE has "wayland? ( opengl )". A Wayland-only image whose toolkit was built without
# the Wayland platform plugin would compile fine and then have nothing to draw on.
#
# opengl and vulkan are ALSO in the target's global USE (make.conf.in, and the desktop profile
# sets both) — so why spell them out? Because the Qt modules bind each other with mirrored
# USE-deps (~dev-qt/qtbase[opengl=,vulkan=,...]), and portage evaluates those against the
# BUILDER's own "/" as well, where our make.conf USE does not apply — the same multi-root
# depgraph quirk documented at length in builder/Dockerfile. mirror_target_pkg_config() copies
# per-package config onto "/", so a flag written HERE reaches both roots; a flag that is only
# global reaches one. Without these lines the graph fails on "/" with a REQUIRED_USE error for
# a constraint the target satisfies perfectly.
dev-qt/qtbase                     libproxy icu wayland opengl vulkan cups
dev-qt/qtdeclarative              opengl vulkan
# qt5compat DEPENDs ~dev-qt/qtbase[icu=], i.e. its own icu must MATCH qtbase's. It defaults off
# and qtbase is on above, so without this the graph splits on the one flag. (qt5compat arrives
# via app-crypt/qca <- kwallet-runtime; it is not something the image asks for directly.)
dev-qt/qt5compat                  icu qml
# Same shape: qttools[widgets] DEPENDs ~dev-qt/qtbase[opengl=]. Note this is NOT the "designer"
# flag — that one is off globally (make.conf.in) and stays off.
dev-qt/qttools                    opengl
dev-qt/qtmultimedia               opengl vulkan qml
dev-qt/qtquick3d                  opengl vulkan
# (qtbase's "cups" above is the Qt half of the printing decision: kde-plasma/print-manager and
# every Qt print dialog resolve through qtbase's CUPS support, and net-print/cups is already in
# @desktop behind the #printing marker.)
# The rest of the same class: every one of these flags is already ON in the target's global
# USE (qml, dbus and X all come from the desktop profile), and every one has to be restated
# per-package so mirror_target_pkg_config() carries it to the builder's "/" too. They are
# listed with the package that needs them so a future reader can tell "the graph needs this"
# from "we chose this" — none of these is a choice.
kde-frameworks/kconfig            qml dbus
kde-frameworks/kcoreaddons        dbus
kde-frameworks/kguiaddons         wayland
kde-frameworks/kidletime          wayland
kde-frameworks/kimageformats      avif
media-libs/libva                  X
kde-frameworks/prison             qml
# Spell checking in KDE text widgets. sonnet[hunspell] (default on) pulls app-text/hunspell,
# which PDEPENDs an app-dicts/myspell-* dictionary per L10N entry — and dies outright here,
# because this image's L10N uses plain "en" while myspell-en's REQUIRED_USE demands one of
# en-AU/en-CA/en-GB/en-US/en-ZA.
# Off rather than fixed by widening L10N, and the precedent is explicit: plan/03 dropped
# gnome-text-editor from the native set specifically to avoid this same tail (hunspell +
# seven myspell-* dictionaries, myspell-de alone ~10 MB compressed). Nothing in this image is
# a text editor — Konsole and Dolphin do not spell-check — so the flag buys a dictionary tree
# on a read-only root for no user-visible function. Text editors are Flatpaks (plan/03), and
# they bring their own dictionaries.
kde-frameworks/sonnet             qml -hunspell -aspell
dev-libs/qcoro                    dbus
sys-apps/dbus                     X
# kde-frameworks/kdnssd wants Avahi's Bonjour-compatibility shim, and it is a real choice
# rather than a mirror artefact: kdnssd is how kio-extras advertises and browses network
# services, which is the same feature smb:// in Dolphin is kept for.
net-dns/avahi                     mdnsresponder-compat
kde-frameworks/kwindowsystem      X wayland
# REQUIRED_USE="vala? ( introspection )", and introspection is off globally now (make.conf.in).
# -vala rather than +introspection for the same reason libportal carries it: .vapi files are
# build-time inputs, which is why stage 50 deletes usr/share/vala outright.
# NB on how libsecret gets here at all: kde-frameworks/kio[kwallet] -> kwallet ->
# kwallet-runtime -> libsecret. That is also the answer to plan/09's open question about
# org.freedesktop.secrets for Flatpak apps — the Secret Service bridge arrives with KWallet.
app-crypt/libsecret               -vala

# These pull in binary packages from the official binhost that were built with a more
# minimal (non-desktop) USE profile than ours; the flags force the matching source builds
# instead of a mismatched binary, per portage's own conflict-resolution suggestions.
# The gtk+/cairo half of this survives the GNOME removal: kde-gtk-config DEPENDs
# x11-libs/gtk+:3[X], which is what keeps GTK3 — and behind it adwaita-icon-theme and
# gnome-base/librsvg — in the image (plan/09 §4).
# gui-libs/gtk (GTK4) is a different matter, and a dry-run depgraph against the pinned tree
# says it LEAVES the image: the bridge needs only :3, breeze-gtk is a pure theme with no
# runtime GTK dep at all, and nothing else in the Plasma set links GTK4. Its line below is
# kept as a no-op until the first real build's expected-packages.txt confirms that — a line
# for an absent package costs nothing, and guessing wrong in the other direction costs a
# rebuild. Delete it (and the vulkan-loader line, whose flags exist for gtk[vulkan]) once the
# generated allowlist agrees.
x11-libs/libdrm             video_cards_radeon
dev-libs/glib               dbus introspection
# vulkan stays explicit even though dev-cpp/gtkmm is gone with gnome-system-monitor: gtk is
# built with vulkan from the global USE, and pinning it here is what stops the two roots
# resolving gtk differently and portage backtracking into "All ebuilds that could satisfy
# >=gui-libs/gtk-4.19.4:4[vulkan?] have been masked".
# "introspection" is dropped from both gtk lines along with the global flag — nothing in a
# Plasma image loads a GObject typelib (see the NB in make.conf.in).
gui-libs/gtk                wayland X vulkan
# gtk[vulkan,X,wayland] requires >=media-libs/vulkan-loader-1.3:=[wayland?,X?], so the loader
# has to carry the same two flags or the graph cannot resolve.
media-libs/vulkan-loader    X wayland
x11-libs/gtk+               wayland X
x11-libs/cairo              X
# kde, not gtk: polkit[gtk] is what pulled gnome-extra/polkit-gnome into the image, and the
# agent this image runs is kde-plasma/polkit-kde-agent. introspection goes with the global
# flag.
sys-auth/polkit             kde -gtk
x11-libs/libxkbcommon       X
media-video/ffmpeg          vaapi
net-print/cups               dbus
x11-base/xwayland           libei
media-libs/libpulse         X
media-plugins/alsa-plugins  pulseaudio
media-libs/freetype         harfbuzz
media-libs/libglvnd         X
# No longer reached by the depgraph under Plasma (it came in behind the GNOME stack); kept as
# a no-op because the flag is a compatibility-API switch other consumers may need.
sys-libs/minizip-ng         compat

# gnupg stays in @base for the sysupdate verify path (plan/03), and app-alternatives/gpg pulls
# app-crypt/pinentry with it. Under GNOME this line read "gtk ... -qt6", because qt6 dragged
# dev-qt/qtbase + kde-frameworks/kwindowsystem into an image that had neither. Both are this
# image's own toolkit now, so the flag inverts: the Qt prompt matches the session, the GTK one
# is dead weight, and ncurses stays as the console fallback.
app-crypt/pinentry          qt6 ncurses -gtk -efl -emacs

# xdg-utils drags dev-perl/Net-DBus + dev-perl/X11-Protocol, and Net-DBus brings XML-Twig and
# the whole LWP/HTML perl stack behind it — ~37 packages. What it buys is the perl
# xdg-screensaver implementation talking X11, which is useless in a Wayland-only session where
# apps inhibit idle through portals. xdg-utils itself stays: it is reachable from the Plasma
# stack the same way it was a hard RDEPEND of gnome-shell and gdm.
#
# -gnome is load-bearing and "-perl" is not the flag that controls this. In 1.2.1-r11 the guard
# reads
#     dbus? ( sys-apps/dbus  gnome? ( dev-perl/Net-DBus  dev-perl/X11-Protocol ) )
#     perl? ( dev-perl/File-MimeInfo )
# so "perl" gates only File-MimeInfo, while the ~37-package LWP/XML stack hangs off gnome —
# and dbus, which this image needs. Keeping -perl is still right (File-MimeInfo is dead weight
# here), but on its own it does not hold that stack out.
#
# Scope of the claim, deliberately narrow: the previous image did NOT ship these packages
# (expected-packages.txt has dev-perl/Parse-Yapp and nothing else), so this is not a
# long-standing leak. It surfaced on a from-scratch rebuild, where xdg-utils resolved to a
# binhost binpkg built with gnome+dbus and its baked RDEPEND pulled Net-DBus in. -gnome makes
# the exclusion deterministic instead of dependent on which binpkg the binhost happens to
# serve. What it costs is xdg-utils' GNOME-specific detection path in xdg-open/xdg-screensaver;
# xdg-open still resolves handlers through gio, and idle inhibition here goes through portals.
x11-misc/xdg-utils          -perl -gnome
# xdg-user-dirs[gtk] (on by default from the desktop profile's global "gtk") pulls
# x11-misc/xdg-user-dirs-gtk, which is banned in package.mask/image — it is the GNOME helper
# that mirrors the XDG directories into GTK's bookmark file. Plasma reads user-dirs.dirs
# itself. The mask caught this at dependency resolution, which is exactly what it is for; the
# fix belongs here, not in the mask.
x11-misc/xdg-user-dirs      -gtk

# Replaces the GNOME-era pair of poppler lines ("cairo" and "-qt6"), whose premises both
# inverted. PDF indexing now runs through kde-frameworks/kfilemetadata[pdf], whose RDEPEND is
# app-text/poppler[qt6(-)] — so qt6 is not merely harmless here, it is required. cairo goes off
# because that backend exists for poppler-glib, whose only consumer was app-misc/localsearch;
# nothing in a Plasma image links the glib frontend. poppler is still a library, not a viewer.
app-text/poppler            qt6 -cairo

# ---- samba, and what it costs --------------------------------------------------------
# This block used to be the GNOME printer-panel cascade: gnome-control-center[cups] pulled
# app-admin/system-config-printer, which brought net-fs/samba for SMB printer discovery and
# dev-python/pillow via docutils. kde-plasma/print-manager talks to CUPS directly, so
# system-config-printer, pillow and the media-libs/tiff[jpeg] line that fed it are all gone.
#
# samba is NOT gone, and its holder is now a deliberate choice rather than a side effect:
# kde-apps/kio-extras[samba], for smb:// browsing in Dolphin. State the consequence here
# rather than leaving it to be rediscovered — samba lists dev-lang/perl:= and
# dev-perl/Parse-Yapp in COMMON_DEPEND with no USE guard, so THE IMAGE STILL SHIPS A PERL
# INTERPRETER. That was already true under GNOME, at one more remove; this change keeps it
# true and makes the reason a direct one. The escape hatch is kio-extras[-samba], which drops
# samba, perl and Parse-Yapp together at the cost of smb:// (plan/06).
net-fs/samba                client
# REQUIRED_USE tiebreak: ngtcp2 wants exactly one crypto backend, and this line appeared
# alongside samba's net-libs/gnutls. Kept for the first Plasma depgraph rather than guessed
# at; drop it if "emerge -p @desktop" resolves without it.
net-libs/ngtcp2             gnutls

# libb2[openmp] RDEPENDs on "|| ( sys-devel/gcc[openmp] llvm-runtimes/clang-runtime[openmp] )"
# for the OpenMP runtime, which portage satisfies by putting a full sys-devel/gcc in the
# image — breaking plan/06's toolchain-free guarantee. The flag is profile-inherited, not
# ours, and it only parallelizes BLAKE2bp hashing. Off.
# The two flags that read exactly backwards now. Under GNOME these were "-qt6", because
# AppStreamQt and libportal-qt6 had no consumer and dragged qtbase in for nothing. Both have a
# consumer here: plasma-workspace RDEPENDs >=dev-libs/appstream-1[qt6] outright when
# appstream is on (which Discover needs), and libportal-qt6 is the Qt portal client binding.
# -gtk and -vala stay: nothing native uses the GTK portal binding, and .vapi files are
# build-time inputs, which is the same reason stage 50 deletes usr/share/vala.
dev-libs/appstream          qt6
# libportal: same inversion on paper, but the dry-run depgraph says the package does not
# arrive at all under Plasma — xdg-desktop-portal-kde does not link it and nothing else asks.
# Left in as a no-op so that if some later Flatpak/portal change does pull it, it arrives with
# the Qt binding rather than the GTK one. -vala for the same reason as the global flag.
dev-libs/libportal          qt6 -gtk -vala
# Still off, for a reason that outlived the "-qt6 everywhere" framing it was written under.
# The flag is "gui", not "qt6": wpa_supplicant[gui] builds wpa_gui and RDEPENDs
# dev-qt/qtbase:6[gui,widgets] plus dev-qt/qtsvg unconditionally. Qt is no longer something
# this image is trying to avoid — but wpa_gui is a standalone supplicant UI, NetworkManager
# owns the network here, and kde-plasma/plasma-nm is the UI, so nothing would ever launch it.
# dbus: NetworkManager drives the supplicant over D-Bus and requires wpa_supplicant[dbus].
# It is on globally for the target and restated here for the builder-root mirror, same as the
# block in the Plasma section.
net-wireless/wpa_supplicant -gui dbus

app-crypt/libb2             -openmp
